From f882f57ad9933705d2d514856fd3c5da21c991fc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: genkigenki <3080081+genkigenki@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 22:48:23 +1000
Subject: [PATCH 077/240] BLE-15: keep the last 64 inbound messages in INLOG.

SMK_INLOG_N changes from 16 to 64.
The reason: the batch task asks for a longer inbound record for the device tests.
The editor reply buffer ed_out changes from 362 B to 732 B, so the reply with 64 entries fits.
The reply format and the readers do not change.
tests/diag_size_test.py now checks that the INLOG reply fits ed_out.
tests/smk_inlog_test.c checks the full ring and the wrap with 64 entries.
tests/inlog_tool_test.py decodes a reply with 64 entries.
Elite size: app code +240 B, static RAM +752 B, pool 0 B.
The code bytes come from a new order of the merged globals, not from new code.
---
 BEHAVIOR.md                  |  2 +-
 firmware/src/app/editor.c    |  2 +-
 firmware/src/app/smk_inlog.h |  2 +-
 tests/diag_size_test.py      | 13 ++++++++++++-
 tests/inlog_tool_test.py     |  4 ++++
 tests/smk_inlog_test.c       | 18 +++++++++++++++---
 web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md       |  2 +-
 7 files changed, 35 insertions(+), 8 deletions(-)

diff --git a/BEHAVIOR.md b/BEHAVIOR.md
index 50c0470..85be01d 100644
--- a/BEHAVIOR.md
+++ b/BEHAVIOR.md
@@ -252,7 +252,7 @@ BLE-11. tools/add_ctl.py and the web installer write the Bluetooth block only wh
 BLE-12. A start record in RAM that a reset keeps (.noinit) protects the power-on. Before the init the app marks the start as trying. A start that does not end READY (not in time, wrong job, not ready), and a power-on that finds the mark still set (the unit crashed, hung or was turned off during the start or in the first 10 s after READY), blocks Bluetooth: the controller is not started at the next power-ons, and STATUS shows FAILED while BLUETOOTH stays ON. So a failing start ends in FAILED, not in a crash at each power-on. 10 s after READY the start counts as good and the mark is cleared. Set BLUETOOTH OFF to clear the block (STATUS OFF); ON again then starts the controller at the next power-on. Turn the unit off only when READY has shown for 10 s. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE ble/ble_glue.h ble_guard_*] [CODE ble.c ble_prepare ble_service plat_ble_set]
 BLE-13. The editor command 33 DIAG (read only, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) returns the crash record, the boot guard counts, the start record of BLE-12 and the live Bluetooth state. The image writes its start stage, its time, its delay count, the last delay's callers and stalls of the tick counter to the mailbox (dbg words). After a reset during the start, the start record also keeps the reset source of that reset. If TIMER4 stands still during the start, core 0 does not wait for ever: the start fails (STATUS FAILED). The first 1 KiB of RAM (0x01C00000) holds no code: it is filled before the start and checked after it, so DIAG shows whether the radio wrote there. The check after the start comes before the first P33 access. Core 0's wait records how it ended (result, TIMER4 stalls, time) in RAM that survives a reset. When the image stops on a library reset request or assert, it records the caller and its heap use (peak, failed allocations). The start record also keeps WLA_CON12 (the crystal trim) before the start and after the init. DIAG also gives the HCI rings (written, read), the host's step, the frames the controller took and gave, the last frame from the controller (type, length, first 6 bytes), and core 0's last Bluetooth tick (time, core 1 poll count), and the rails. Editor command BT_TEST op 3 returns the last boot's timer 5 frame watch; op 4 returns core 1's records (core 0 seen stopping, the RAM canary). The mailbox and host values are raw: after a reset they are the last start's. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE app/editor.c ED_DIAG] [CODE ble.c ble_diag] [CODE ../../ble/ctl/src/ctl_main.c stage]
 BLE-14. The editor command 34 BT_TEST (SMK-37 with Bluetooth, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) is for tools/ble_autotest.py. Op 1 clears the start block as BLUETOOTH OFF does; the BLUETOOTH setting does not change. Op 2 restarts the unit 200 ms after the reply (a chip reset, as after a crash). It never starts the update loader. (Added 2026-10-15.) [CODE app/editor.c ED_BT_TEST] [CODE app/main_fm1.c]
-BLE-15. The editor command 38 INLOG (SMK-37, read only, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) is the inbound recorder. The firmware keeps the last 16 inbound clip messages in a ring: the time, the source (0 USB, 1 Bluetooth, 3 Bluetooth while notify is off, no cell change), the status and the two data bytes. Source 3 is a Bluetooth note for a clip cell while notify is off: the cell did not change (CLP-18). The ring also keeps each change of the Bluetooth link (source 2: connected, notify on). It keeps only note-on and note-off messages for notes 0-98. Argument 0 also returns the 40 clip velocities and channels. Argument 1 returns the 16 pad colours in the pad colour store and the 16 pad colours in the LED driver data. tools/ble_autotest.py --diag-only shows them as text and writes MISMATCH when a pad does not agree with its cell. (Added 2026-10-16, a76.) [CODE app/smk_inlog.h] [CODE app/main_fm1.c smk_inlog_reply] [CODE ../../tools/inlog_fmt.py] [TEST tests/smk_inlog_test.c]
+BLE-15. The editor command 38 INLOG (SMK-37, read only, web/EDITOR_PROTOCOL.md) is the inbound recorder. The firmware keeps the last 64 inbound clip messages in a ring (16 before 2026-10-16 23:00): the time, the source (0 USB, 1 Bluetooth, 3 Bluetooth while notify is off, no cell change), the status and the two data bytes. Source 3 is a Bluetooth note for a clip cell while notify is off: the cell did not change (CLP-18). The ring also keeps each change of the Bluetooth link (source 2: connected, notify on). It keeps only note-on and note-off messages for notes 0-98. Argument 0 also returns the 40 clip velocities and channels. Argument 1 returns the 16 pad colours in the pad colour store and the 16 pad colours in the LED driver data. tools/ble_autotest.py --diag-only shows them as text and writes MISMATCH when a pad does not agree with its cell. (Added 2026-10-16, a76.) The reply with 64 entries has 732 bytes. The editor reply buffer (editor.c ed_out) has 732 bytes (tests/diag_size_test.py). [CODE app/smk_inlog.h] [CODE app/main_fm1.c smk_inlog_reply] [CODE ../../tools/inlog_fmt.py] [TEST tests/smk_inlog_test.c]
 BLE-16. When a BLE link ends, X0X sends All Notes Off, so no note hangs. [USER 2026-10-16] Not in code on integration 110d4c2. CLP-70 ends only the clip keys notes at a Bluetooth disconnect. (Was BLE-12 on smk37 before 2026-10-16 21:53. BLE-15 is the inbound recorder on ble-pad-a76.)
 
 ## 15. Clip mode (APC40 mkII emulation for Loopy Pro), SMK-37 only
diff --git a/firmware/src/app/editor.c b/firmware/src/app/editor.c
index cd31636..19c1185 100644
--- a/firmware/src/app/editor.c
+++ b/firmware/src/app/editor.c
@@ -30,7 +30,7 @@ static void smk_inlog_reply(uint32_t arg);   /* main_fm1.c */
 static uint32_t ed_reboot_at;                /* fm1_ms + 1 when a restart was asked for (0: none) */
 #define ED_READ_MAX 224u                     /* X0X: SMP_READ's bytes a frame (the break page draws the slots) */
 
-static uint8_t ed_out[362];
+static uint8_t ed_out[732];                  /* ED_INLOG with 64 entries: 732 B (tests/diag_size_test.py) */
 static uint32_t ed_n;
 
 static void ed_begin(uint32_t cmd)
diff --git a/firmware/src/app/smk_inlog.h b/firmware/src/app/smk_inlog.h
index a8606aa..ac91051 100644
--- a/firmware/src/app/smk_inlog.h
+++ b/firmware/src/app/smk_inlog.h
@@ -8,7 +8,7 @@
 #pragma once
 #include <stdint.h>
 
-#define SMK_INLOG_N 16u
+#define SMK_INLOG_N 64u       /* at most 127: INLOG sends the count as one 7-bit byte */
 #define SMK_INLOG_CELLS 40u     /* the clip cells that INLOG returns (SMK_APC40_CLIP_COUNT) */
 enum { SMK_INLOG_USB = 0, SMK_INLOG_BLE = 1, SMK_INLOG_EDGE = 2, SMK_INLOG_NOCELL = 3 };
 /* SMK_INLOG_EDGE: d2 = the link state, bit 0 the link is up, bit 1 notify is on. */
diff --git a/tests/diag_size_test.py b/tests/diag_size_test.py
index f52dd72..5115d2a 100644
--- a/tests/diag_size_test.py
+++ b/tests/diag_size_test.py
@@ -62,5 +62,16 @@ reply = 5 + 1 + 5 * total + 1          # header with cmd, count, 5 bytes for eac
 if reply > out:
     print(f"FAIL ED_DIAG: the reply has {reply} bytes, but ed_out has {out}")
     fails += 1
-print(f"{'FAIL' if fails else 'PASS'} ED_DIAG writes stay in w[] and ed_out ({fails} fail)")
+inlog = (ROOT / "firmware/src/app/smk_inlog.h").read_text()
+n_in = int(re.search(r"#define SMK_INLOG_N (\d+)u", inlog).group(1))
+cells = int(re.search(r"#define SMK_INLOG_CELLS (\d+)u", inlog).group(1))
+reply = 5 + 1 + 5 + 5 * 2 * n_in + 2 * cells + 1   # header with cmd, count, now, 2 words for each entry, cells, F7
+print(f"INLOG reply: {n_in} entries, {reply} bytes, ed_out {out}")
+if n_in > 127:
+    print(f"FAIL ED_INLOG: {n_in} entries, but the count is one 7-bit byte")
+    fails += 1
